---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
---

<Layout title="Guide · mailypoppins" description="Install mailypoppins and send your first email in under 5 minutes.">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content" class="content page">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Guide</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Install mailypoppins and send your first email in under 5 minutes.</p>
    </header>

    <!-- Installation -->
    <section>
      <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>

      <h3>Homebrew (macOS and Linux)</h3>
      <p>
        Installs a prebuilt binary, with no Rust toolchain needed.
        The full name taps the repository and trusts only this formula, which Homebrew 6 requires for any third-party tap:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="bash"
        code={`brew install sylvainHellin/mailypoppins/mailypoppins`}
      />

      <h3>Prebuilt binaries</h3>
      <p>
        Download the archive for your platform (macOS arm64, Linux x86_64 gnu or
        static musl) from the
        <a href="https://github.com/sylvainHellin/mailypoppins/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub Releases page</a>,
        verify the <code>.sha256</code> checksum, and place the
        <code>mp</code> binary somewhere on your <code>PATH</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="mac-app">Mac app</h3>
      <p>
        <em>From 0.11.0.</em> Download
        <code>mailypoppins-desktop-aarch64-apple-darwin.dmg</code> (Apple
        silicon only) from the Releases page and drag <code>mailypoppins.app</code>
        to Applications. The app carries its own <code>mp</code> and starts
        the daemon with it, so it needs nothing else installed. It is not
        signed yet: after the first blocked launch, allow it under System
        Settings, Privacy &amp; Security ("Open Anyway"), or clear the
        quarantine flag:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="bash"
        code={`xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/mailypoppins.app`}
      />
      <p>
        To use the app's <code>mp</code> from a terminal too, link it onto
        your <code>PATH</code> instead of installing a second copy, so the
        CLI, the TUI and the app share one version of the daemon:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="bash"
        code={`ln -s /Applications/mailypoppins.app/Contents/MacOS/mp /usr/local/bin/mp`}
      />
      <p>
        Later versions arrive in the app. Once a day it checks the Releases
        page, and when a newer version is out, an "Update to" entry appears
        at the foot of the sidebar. Choosing it downloads the update, checks
        its signature and puts it in place while you keep working; "Restart
        now" then switches to it, daemon included. "Check for updates" in the
        command palette, the App menu and Settings checks at once. The
        <code>mp</code> link above follows the update, since it points into
        the app.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a daemon of another version is already running, the app says so
        and offers to restart it with its own. To uninstall, run
        <code>mp daemon stop</code>, quit the app, and delete it and the
        link; your mail and <code>config.toml</code> stay where
        <code>mp config path</code> says.
      </p>

      <h3>From source</h3>
      <p>
        You need the Rust toolchain installed. If you don't have it yet, install it from
        <a href="https://rustup.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rustup.rs</a>.
        Then clone the repository and build:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="bash"
        code={`git clone https://github.com/sylvainHellin/mailypoppins
cd mailypoppins
cargo install --path .`}
      />
      <p>
        This installs the <code>mp</code> binary to <code>~/.cargo/bin/</code>.
        Make sure that directory is in your <code>PATH</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Setup -->
    <section>
      <h2>Setup</h2>
      <p>Run the interactive setup wizard:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="mp config init" />
      <p>The wizard walks through these steps:</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Choose a provider: standard IMAP/SMTP, Microsoft 365 with OAuth2, or Graph API (Graph signs in through the built-in mailypoppins app, so press Enter at the client ID and tenant prompts; until the mailypoppins publisher is verified with Microsoft, an administrator of your organisation has to approve the app at the first sign-in, which personal Microsoft accounts do not need).</li>
        <li>Enter credentials (server host, port, username, email address).</li>
        <li>Automatic connection test to verify everything works.</li>
        <li>Mailbox discovery: the wizard lists server mailboxes and lets you assign the inbox, archive, and sent roles; "Type another name" takes a mailbox the server did not list, and a role with nothing left to pick asks for the name directly.</li>
        <li>Drafts, trash and junk: left empty, the daemon uses the folders the server marks; name one only when the server does not.</li>
        <li>Policy overrides: optional lines such as <code>Labels/* ignore</code>, one per prompt, until an empty answer. Every other folder is found and synced without being listed here.</li>
        <li>Config is written to <code>~/.config/mailypoppins/config.toml</code>.</li>
        <li>Password is stored securely in the OS keyring (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, or Linux Secret Service).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For Microsoft 365 accounts, the wizard runs the OAuth2 device code flow: it
        displays a URL and a code, you sign in via your browser, and the token is
        cached locally.
        It then tests the SMTP login with that token: a tenant that disables SMTP AUTH
        refuses it, and the wizard warns and goes on, since only sending needs it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The wizard does not ask about signatures. A signature is a Markdown file in
        <code>~/.config/mailypoppins/signatures/</code>: press <code>cs</code> in the TUI to
        create one and make it the account's default, or write the file by hand. See
        <a href="/config/#signatures-section">Signatures</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- First Sync -->
    <section>
      <h2>First Sync</h2>
      <p>After setup, pull your emails from the server:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="mp sync" />
      <p>
        This syncs every folder the account keeps: the inbox, sent and archive folders, every folder you created on the server, and the newest messages of the trash and junk folders.
        Each message becomes a row in the account's local store
        (<code>store.sqlite3</code>), with its text, its HTML and its attachments kept
        beside it as content-addressed blobs. Nothing is written as a mail file tree:
        the Markdown you read in the TUI is rendered from the row on demand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sync is additive by default: it only downloads new messages, deduplicating by
        Message-ID. The mailbox is enumerated in full every pass, so messages the server
        no longer lists are pruned from the local store however old they are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Launch the TUI -->
    <section>
      <h2>Launch the TUI</h2>
      <p>Run the binary with no arguments to open the interactive terminal UI:</p>
      <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="mp" />
      <p>
        The TUI shows a sidebar with your mailboxes, an email list, and a headers/body
        preview pane. Core navigation:
      </p>
      <ul class="key-list">
        <li><code>j</code> / <code>k</code> to move up and down the email list</li>
        <li><code>1</code>-<code>9</code> to jump to a mailbox by index</li>
        <li><code>Enter</code> to read the selected message in the body pane, where <code>j</code> / <code>k</code> scroll it</li>
        <li><code>l</code> / <code>h</code> to expand and collapse the conversation under the cursor</li>
        <li><code>e</code> to open the selected row in your <code>$EDITOR</code>: a draft opens for editing, a received message opens as a read-only Markdown copy</li>
        <li><code>/</code> to filter, <code>\</code> to search (includes body text)</li>
        <li><code>?</code> for the full help overlay</li>
        <li><code>q</code> to quit</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The read-only copy is rendered from the local store on the spot, with the headers
        as YAML frontmatter and the body below them, and it is deleted again when you leave
        the editor. It is there for searching, folding and copying a long message with the
        tools you already know. Edits go nowhere: the store is the source of truth, and
        nothing reads the copy back.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each mailbox but Drafts lists one row per conversation: the question in your inbox, your
        replies in Sent and the draft of your next reply are one row. The row takes the date of
        its newest message and shows the message count, such as <code>(3)</code>. The first
        <code>Enter</code> on a conversation of several messages expands it in place, newest
        first, each member labelled with the mailbox it lives in and its sender. Your own messages,
        those from your account's address in whatever mailbox and everything in Sent, read
        "Me" and carry a tinted mailbox label; a draft reads "Draft". The next <code>Enter</code> reads the conversation, and
        <code>y</code> on a member copies its selector (in the desktop app, a draft's file path). Archive, delete, flag and mark read on a
        collapsed row act on its messages in the mailbox you are looking at, never on your
        Sent copies. Press <code>tc</code> to list an account one row per message instead. The
        TUI remembers the choice in <code>~/.config/mailypoppins/tui.json</code>. In the desktop
        app, <code>tc</code> works too, and so does the "Conversation view" switch on the
        account's Settings card.
      </p>
      <p>
        The marker column on the left of the list carries one glyph per message: a dot
        for unread, a reply arrow once you have answered it, a forward arrow once you
        have forwarded it, and nothing for a message you have only read. Answered and
        forwarded are kept in step with the server, so a reply you sent from your phone
        shows up here too.
      </p>
      <p>
        The TUI watches your inbox in the background (via IMAP IDLE or Graph polling)
        and fetches new mail automatically when it arrives.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Your First Draft -->
    <section>
      <h2>Your First Draft</h2>
      <p>
        The draft workflow goes through three stages: <code>draft</code> (writing),
        <code>approved</code> (reviewed), and <code>sent</code> (delivered). Here is
        the end-to-end flow from the CLI:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="bash"
        code={`# Create a new draft. It prints the draft's selector:
# mp://work/drafts/9f2c1ab30d5e4471
mp new meeting-followup

# Open it in your editor and fill in recipients, subject, body
mp edit mp://work/drafts/9f2c1ab30d5e4471

# Mark it as reviewed and ready to send
mp mark-approved mp://work/drafts/9f2c1ab30d5e4471

# Send it (the id alone works too, the rest is filled in from your config)
mp send 9f2c1ab30d5e4471`}
      />
      <p>Or from the TUI:</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Press <code>cn</code> to create a new draft. It opens in your <code>$EDITOR</code>.</li>
        <li>Write your email in Markdown with YAML frontmatter (to, subject, body).</li>
        <li>Save and close the editor. The draft appears in the list.</li>
        <li>Press <code>x</code> to send. The draft is approved and sent in one step; confirm when prompted.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A draft file looks like this:</p>
      <CodeBlock
        lang="yaml"
        filename="meeting-followup.md"
        code={`---
to: colleague@example.com
cc: team@example.com
subject: "Meeting follow-up"
status: draft
---

Hi,

Here are the action items from today's meeting:

- Item one
- Item two

Let me know if I missed anything.`}
      />
    </section>

    <!-- What's Next -->
    <section>
      <h2>What's Next</h2>
      <ul class="next-links">
        <li><a href="/commands/">Commands</a>: full reference for every CLI command</li>
        <li><a href="/draft-format/">Draft Format</a>: frontmatter fields, status workflow, attachments</li>
        <li><a href="/config/">Configuration</a>: multi-account setup, OAuth2, Graph API, signatures</li>
        <li><a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>: troubleshooting and common questions</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding-top: var(--space-16);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }

  .page-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-12);
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .steps {
    list-style: decimal;
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .steps li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .key-list {
    list-style: disc;
    padding-left: var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .key-list li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .next-links {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .next-links li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .next-links a {
    color: var(--color-link);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .next-links a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-text);
  }
</style>
